		{
			"id": "epRzzWqYAywY3lG76",
			"description": "Hexagrammic Ward Charm",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A small charm inscribed with hexagrammic wards, worn by Imperial soldiers and priests operating anywhere a warp-taint might be encountered — a cheaper, weaker cousin of the wards painted across whole ship-hulls and chapels to keep daemons at bay. Standard Imperial Creed practice, referenced throughout Black Library and codified in Dark Heresy/Only War's cheap ward-item rules.\n\nDesign: A minor +1 conditional bonus, priced and weighted as a trinket rather than real equipment — protection at the margin, not a substitute for a Null Rod or a trained anti-psyker.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Psyker"
			],
			"tech_level": "10^",
			"legality_class": "1",
			"base_value": "500",
			"base_weight": "0.5 lb",
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "conditional_modifier",
					"situation": "to resist possession and Warp-based Affliction directed at the wearer",
					"amount": 1
				}
			],
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eJJpia-etC4bcZWeg",
			"description": "Psyocculum (Psy-Track)",
			"local_notes": "A worn ocular rig that lets a non-psyker see psychic residue and warp-taint the naked eye cannot: roll vs Electronics Operation (Sensors) or a relevant Hidden Lore specialisation to spot a hidden psyker, daemon, or recently-used Warp Sorcery-tagged ability's afterglow within 20 yards. Functions as a wearable, skill-based substitute for the Witch- sight advantage, usable by anyone trained on it, not only psykers.\n\nLore: Inquisitorial witch-hunters and Officio Assassinorum operatives use devices exactly like this to track psykers who have no wish to be found — the \"psyocculum\" name and function are best known from Rogue Trader/Dark Heresy-line Inquisitorial wargear lists; Black Library depictions of witch-hunting technology (Eisenhorn's own gadgetry) are consistent with the concept without naming the device.\n\nDesign: Left as a skill-roll rules note rather than a coded feature, since it grants a new use of an existing skill (a sensory check) rather than a numeric bonus — the same approach the build tool's `notes` field is meant for.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
